Monday: Thesis Anatomy
A proposal should make one connected argument: the question matters, the existing evidence leaves a defensible opening, and the proposed method can address that opening.

Proposal Map
Today we are studying the architecture of the Full Thesis Proposal, the substantial document you are writing for this course. This is different from the UHP Thesis Proposal Form, which is a short administrative submission containing a title, brief prospectus, and committee chair.
For a working draft or approved example, identify:

the problem or motivating context;
the research question;
the literature-based rationale;
the proposed method;
the expected contribution; and
the assumptions connecting those parts.

The full proposal normally includes an abstract, introduction, literature review, methods, expected results, discussion/significance, conclusion, and references. Your department may use different headings or require additional material.
Mark any point where the logic depends on information that has not been provided.

