Method and Feasibility Blueprint
Week 6, due Wed, Oct 14. A concise design memo that carries the methods and evidence work of Weeks 5 and 6 into your proposal. Use headings. A table is fine. Length is not the target; reviewable decisions are.
Include:

your current research question and the quantitative, qualitative, mixed, or discipline-specific design it requires;
the unit, population, system, cases, or materials you will study;
the evidence or data you need and how you expect to obtain it;
the collection, measurement, comparison, and analysis steps you can name at this stage;
feasibility constraints, including access, time, equipment, sample or case availability, permissions, safety, cost, and ethics or human-subjects review when applicable;
one important risk and a feasible alternative if the preferred design does not work;
one published claim whose wording you would narrow to match its evidence; and
one specific design question for your Primary Advisor.

Mark an item “not applicable” and explain why when it genuinely does not fit your field. Do not invent details to fill a box.
The blueprint is satisfactory when a reader can trace the question to the proposed evidence and analysis, identify the largest feasibility constraint, and see what still requires advisor or specialist judgment. The claim example must show both the original wording and a narrowed version you would accept.






NoteFall Graduation Track



Apply the same structure to completed thesis work. Submit a Thesis Method, Analysis, and Feasibility Audit naming the design actually used, data or evidence obtained, analysis complete and outstanding, ethics or permissions status when applicable, the strongest supported claim, at least one claim needing qualification, the largest completion risk, and the dated response to that risk.

Literature Synthesis Draft
Week 8, due by the Canvas deadline on Wed, Oct 28. We do not meet that day. Complete the independent drafting and self-diagnosis sequence on the Week 8 page before submitting. This is the first substantive draft and the first to receive detailed written feedback.
A literature review is not a sequence of article summaries. It is an argument about what is known, how it is known, where findings converge, and what remains uncertain. The synthesis matrix built on Monday is the tool; this draft is the result.
Satisfactory means the draft:

includes the search record and synthesis matrix as supporting material;
explains the source-set boundary, including databases or starting sources, major terms or citation chains, inclusion reasoning, and unresolved evidence;
uses traceable citations to sources the writer has read closely enough to describe their method, finding, and limitation;
organizes sources around themes rather than one source per paragraph;
states at least one pattern that holds across multiple sources;
names a disagreement or limitation and explains why it matters for your question; and
identifies a gap without claiming that no prior work exists.

That last one is where drafts most often fail. “No one has studied this” is almost never true and is easy for a committee to disprove. “These studies address X in system A, but not in system B, where the mechanism may differ” is defensible.






NoteFall Graduation Track



Use your thesis sources and existing literature-review draft. Submit a Revised Thesis Literature Synthesis that reorganizes at least one section around patterns, disagreements, or limitations rather than one-source-at-a-time summary.
